e  attack face terror-related charges  prosecutors say they helped the driver  obtain weapons and rent the truck the  five others are accused of arms  trafficking and helping indirectly  says some of the families hope the trial  helps them move on in their healing but  he says others are still too angry for  it to make a difference  there's also disappointment the man who  was behind the wheel never faced justice  mike armstrong global news  i had how french language issues could  tip the balance in the next federal  election  more than half of quebec residents  believe the use of french across the  country is under threat that's according  to internal government polling  commissioned by the prime minister's  office and obtained by global news that  belief alone may pose a threat to the  liberal government's chances of winning  the next election our chief political  correspondent david aiken explains  days before the beginning of the quebec  provincial election statistics canada  had some bad news the latest census data  revealed that the proportion of french  speakers has declined everywhere in  canada including in quebec that's a data  point that pushed language to the center  of that election yes absolutely so  language is uh  always a significant issue in quebec  incumbent premier francois lego is miles  ahead in the polls and so if he needs  someone to blame about the decline of  french well it will not be his  provincial opponents the answer is  justin trudeau right and it's the  federal government jan plant was a top  political aid to ministers in the harper  government and he's played key roles in  fighting federal campaigns in quebec  what i expect that we will see primarily  ago more and more talking about  justin trudeau and the federal  government and asking quebecers for  another strong mandate so that we can  continue to be strong you know facing  ottawa to protect our language protect  our identity  the trudeau liberals have won three  elections largely based on strong  support in quebec but if that quebec  support waivers and it could over  language policy well that's trouble for  the liberals they can't afford to start  losing ground in francophones  communities not only in quebec but all  across canada and could make victory for  the conservatives easier federal  liberals are  are vulnerable on language and on quebec  identity more generally that's why the  internal polling from last april was  crucial for the trudeau government to  ensure that they are on the right track  with what quebecers want done to boost  french language use in the rest of the  country  sixty percent of quebecers want  increased funding for post-secondary  institutions outside of quebec that  offer instruction in french  sixty percent of quebecers want more  money for french immersion in the rest  of the country and fifty six percent  want funding to help entrepreneurs do  business in french in the rest of canada  the anxiety about the future of french  not only in quebec but elsewhere in  canada is something that is you know  featured in that data the poll also said  65 percent of quebecers said  guaranteeing the equality of french and  english right across the country should  be a top federal priority the trudeau  government promised to do that with an  overhaul to the official languages act  and that overhaul will be on  parliament's agenda when mps return to  the house of commons later this month  david aiken global news ottawa  next a new library for vancouver that's  about so much more than books  with control her shot high her  condolences to our southern neighbors  tonight after team canada beat team usa  again for the top prize at hockey's  women's world championship final the two  rival women's teams faced off in a  rematch after canada previously  outscored the americans both in last  year's final and the olympic gold medal  game earlier this year canada's women  came out victorious once more tonight  with a final score of two to one  a brand new library has just opened its  doors in vancouver and it's like no  other the city has ever seen it's called  the vancouver black library and the ubc  student behind the project says it's  bigger than books she hopes the new  space will start a new chapter for the  city's black community paul johnson  reports  it was dark stairway but your face  lights up soaking up the ambience in  vancouver's newest culture space for  maya preshon this is a dream turned  reality it happened definitely  sooner than i could have imagined  rewind back to the middle of winter when  maya told us about her vision of a  library that would tell the story of  vancouver's black community at the time  she had no location no funding just the  moxie of a university student who wanted  to do something and what a doer she is  in the 70s there was a neighborhood that  was a community hub for black people in  vancouver and that was hogan's alley and  it was dismantled to make way for city  infrastructure  overlooked and unknown to many east  vancouver's hogan's alley at its roots  in the black men who worked as stewards  on the trains that came to nearby  pacific central station  jimi hendrix's grandmother lived there  and musicians like nat king cole and  louis armstrong would eat at its  restaurants when they came through town  the city's role in demolishing it for  the georgia viaduct is now a lesson in  what not to do it was the kind of tenor  of the time that was just how things  they viewed inner cities as blight they  viewed ethnic enclaves of something to  get rid of not something to embrace  hogan's alley is just one of the stories  you can learn about at the vancouver  black library now operating in a  building full of art spaces in chinatown  everyone is welcome and you'll find  books by black authors about all kinds  of black stories all available thanks to  maya and her volunteers  and finished in time so she can get back  to class at ubc it's all just crowd  funding crowd support knowledge from the  community contributions from the  community what are the chances we'll be  hearing about maya preston again  paul johnson global news  what an accomplishment that's global  national for this sunday night i'm  colleen christie tonight's york canada  is this rainbow near agassi bc we love  seeing your canada please keep emailing  your photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  thanks for watching eric sorenson will  be at the anchor desk tomorrow good  night  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

